window.TANDEM_DOCS_INDEX = [{"title": "Installation", "page": "Installation", "url": "/tandem/docs/installation/", "text": "Installation You need: a Linux x86-64 workstation with an NVIDIA GPU, CUDA 12, ffmpeg and about 25 GB of free disk; a Franka FR3 or Panda with a Robotiq 2F-85 gripper, and its polymetis NUC; a wrist ZED camera, 1 third-person ZED camera and the ZED SDK ; pipx or uv , and a Gemini API key ; for human phases, a VR headset ( Meta Quest ). tandem builds the teleop driver's environment itself ( Teleope"}, {"title": "Install", "page": "Installation", "url": "/tandem/docs/installation/#install", "text": "pipx install git+https://github.com/SamratSahoo/TANDEM.git # or, with uv: uv tool install git+https://github.com/SamratSahoo/TANDEM.git"}, {"title": "Initialize", "page": "Installation", "url": "/tandem/docs/installation/#initialize", "text": "Before you start, install the ZED SDK and plug in the cameras. Then run: tandem init It walks through the setup, in this order: The robot: the NUC's address and the arm type. TiPToP 's runtime, which it builds (5\u201320 minutes). The perception servers TiPToP calls, M2T2 and FoundationStereo , which it builds too ( Perception servers ). The cameras. It lists the ZED cameras it finds and suggests a ser"}, {"title": "Check the setup", "page": "Installation", "url": "/tandem/docs/installation/#check-the-setup", "text": "tandem doctor # every check, and what to do about each; --no-hardware skips the robot, camera and server probes Common problems are in Troubleshooting ."}, {"title": "Robot setup", "page": "Robot setup", "url": "/tandem/docs/robot/", "text": "Robot setup The NUC runs two programs: DROID 's server, which drives the arm and gripper through polymetis (teleop uses it), and TiPToP 's shim , which the planner uses to control the arm. Do steps 1\u20134 on the NUC. Install DROID. Follow DROID's NUC guide ( Docker or host ), using the TANDEM branch of the fork: git clone -b TANDEM --recurse-submodules https://github.com/SamratSahoo/droid.git Its \"Co"}, {"title": "Perception servers", "page": "Perception servers", "url": "/tandem/docs/perception-servers/", "text": "Perception servers TiPToP asks two servers on every rollout: M2T2 for grasps and FoundationStereo for depth. tandem init builds both, with torch compiled for your GPU and their model weights. You don't need to start them yourself: tandem collect starts any server that isn't running before the session warms up, and stops the ones it started when the session ends. tandem servers status # installed? "}, {"title": "Cameras and calibration", "page": "Cameras and calibration", "url": "/tandem/docs/cameras/", "text": "Cameras and calibration You can configure camera settings through TANDEM: tandem rig show # the robot, the cameras, and which have extrinsics tandem rig set robot.type panda_robotiq # a Panda tandem rig set cameras.external_2.serial SERIAL # roles: hand, external, external_2 Extrinsics. Every configured camera needs extrinsics before a session can start. tandem rig show and tandem doctor list the "}, {"title": "Teleoperation", "page": "Teleoperation", "url": "/tandem/docs/teleop/", "text": "Teleoperation A person carries out human phases by driving the arm with a Meta Quest VR controller. tandem builds the teleop driver's environment for you. tandem executors install teleop # builds the teleop driver's environment and turns teleop on tandem executors list # teleop should say `ready` tandem init offers to do this for you. The install fetches the workstation side of DROID and builds a "}, {"title": "Your first collection", "page": "Your first collection", "url": "/tandem/docs/getting-started/", "text": "Your first collection After setup ( Installation through Teleoperation ), collecting data takes four steps: choose a task, check the plan, collect, then review and export."}, {"title": "Choose a task", "page": "Your first collection", "url": "/tandem/docs/getting-started/#choose-a-task", "text": "tandem profile use store-bread-in-closed-box A profile is one task in one YAML file, ~/tandem-data/profiles/<name>.yml . tandem init added the paper's five, each with the settings the paper used: store-bread-in-closed-box , cover-bread-rolls , solve-constrained-puzzle , sort-and-cover-snacks and open-obstructed-book . To make your own with the paper's settings: tandem profile create my-task --prom"}, {"title": "Check the plan", "page": "Your first collection", "url": "/tandem/docs/getting-started/#check-the-plan", "text": "tandem plan \"place the bread inside the box\" --image workspace.png This needs only the photo and the Gemini key . It prints the phases, who does each, each human phase's magic operator and the invented predicates. -o LABEL (repeatable) pins object labels. If part of the task can't be planned, it says so. Usually an object wasn't detected; put it on the table or reword the task."}, {"title": "Collect", "page": "Your first collection", "url": "/tandem/docs/getting-started/#collect", "text": "tandem collect # in the terminal tandem ui # or in the browser, at http://127.0.0.1:8787 tandem collect also takes --episodes N , --task \"...\" , --no-execute (plan only) and --no-record . The session warms the planner once, then waits for you. The footer shows the keys each state accepts: state keys task prompt Enter runs the task, n types a new one planning or executing p preempts, t lends you th"}, {"title": "Review and export", "page": "Your first collection", "url": "/tandem/docs/getting-started/#review-and-export", "text": "tandem traj list # newest first; --status eval|success|failure tandem traj relabel <id> failure # move one between success, failure and eval tandem export lerobot --repo <hf-user>/my-task # LeRobot v3.0 from success/, in ~/tandem-data/exports/ tandem export lerobot --repo <hf-user>/my-task --push # and upload it Each trial becomes one episode, with its robot and human legs merged ( on-disk format "}, {"title": "Collecting", "page": "Collecting", "url": "/tandem/docs/collecting/", "text": "Collecting A collection session runs trials of one task, one after another: the robot plans and executes its phases, you teleoperate the human ones, and each trial is labeled and filed. This page covers what you can do at each step. tandem collect # the active profile, in the terminal tandem collect my-task -n 20 # another profile; stop after 20 trials tandem collect --task \"stack the cups\" # a di"}, {"title": "Planning from a photo", "page": "Planning from a photo", "url": "/tandem/docs/planning/", "text": "Planning from a photo tandem plan shows how a task will be split into robot and human phases, from one photo of the workspace, before you collect. It needs no robot, GPU or planner runtime. tandem plan \"place the bread inside the box\" --image workspace.png -o bread -o box -o plate This previews a task's phases before you collect. It prints who does each phase, each phase's goal or magic operator, "}, {"title": "From Python", "page": "Planning from a photo", "url": "/tandem/docs/planning/#from-python", "text": "import tandem plan = tandem . plan_task ( \"place the bread inside the box\" , \"workspace.png\" , objects = [ \"bread\" , \"box\" , \"plate\" ]) for phase in plan . phases : print ( phase . executor , phase . description , phase . atoms ) plan_task returns a PhasePlan with .phases , .spec and .to_json() . Each phase has .executor , .description and .atoms . The image can be a path, a PIL image or an RGB ui"}, {"title": "Reviewing and exporting", "page": "Reviewing and exporting", "url": "/tandem/docs/reviewing/", "text": "Reviewing and exporting Every trial is filed under success/ , failure/ or eval/ . These commands list, replay and refile them, and export the successes as a LeRobot dataset. tandem traj list -s success -n 0 # every success, newest first tandem traj open <id> # replay one in its planner's viewer (TiPToP: Rerun) tandem traj relabel <id> failure # move it to success, failure or eval <id> is a traject"}, {"title": "Exporting", "page": "Reviewing and exporting", "url": "/tandem/docs/reviewing/#exporting", "text": "tandem export lerobot --repo <owner>/<name> # writes ~/tandem-data/exports/<owner>/<name> tandem export lerobot --repo <owner>/<name> --push # and uploads it tandem export manifest --out index.json # a JSON index of the trajectories (default: stdout) export lerobot writes a LeRobot v3.0 dataset in lerobot/droid_1.0.1 's schema, for \u03c0\u2080.\u2085-DROID fine-tuning. Each run is logged to export.log ( logs )."}, {"title": "Web UI", "page": "Web UI", "url": "/tandem/docs/web-ui/", "text": "Web UI The web UI does everything the terminal does, plus video review of every trajectory. tandem ui # http://127.0.0.1:8787, or the next free port tandem ui --port 9000 --no-open The UI needs no Node, CDN, GPU or robot. ui.host , ui.port and ui.open_browser set its defaults. Ctrl-C lets running sessions park the arm and finish merging. A second Ctrl-C quits at once and leaves the arm where it is"}, {"title": "HTTP API", "page": "Web UI", "url": "/tandem/docs/web-ui/#http-api", "text": "curl http://127.0.0.1:8787/api/rig curl -X PATCH http://127.0.0.1:8787/api/rig -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ -d '{\"robot.host\": \"NUC_ADDRESS\", \"cameras.external_2\": null}' route returns or does GET /api/planners[/{name}] , GET /api/executors Same as planners list\\|info --json and executors list --json . Add ?profile=P for another profile. POST /api/planners/{name}/use\\|default , POST /api/"}, {"title": "Configuration", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/", "text": "Configuration This page lists every setting tandem reads. For setup, see Installation ; for commands, the Command reference ."}, {"title": "Profiles", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#profiles", "text": "A profile is one task in one YAML file: # ~/tandem-data/profiles/my-task.yml version : 3 description : 'Cup onto plate' task : prompt : put the cup on the plate # the episode's language label target_episodes : 30 hitl : # phase planning (see below) enabled : true on_verification_failure : label planner : backend : tiptop options : tamp : # TiPToP's task settings num_particles : 512 recording : ena"}, {"title": "Profile keys", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#profile-keys", "text": "key default what it does version 3 Layout version. See older profiles . description Free text. The profile's name is its file name: lowercase letters, digits, - and _ , not starting with - or _ . task.prompt The episode's language label, and what phase planning splits into steps. task.goal null The planner's goal, when it must differ from task.prompt . task.target_episodes 20 Target shown by profi"}, {"title": "The paper's five", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#the-papers-five", "text": "tandem init adds the paper's five tasks, each with the settings the paper collected it with, plus the three switches . profile paper task (Fig. 3) its own settings cover-bread-rolls Cover Bread Rolls A grasp-centre cost and threshold, and finer voxels. solve-constrained-puzzle Solve Constrained Puzzle Surface-fitted placement . sort-and-cover-snacks Sort & Cover Snacks open-obstructed-book Open Ob"}, {"title": "Older profiles", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#older-profiles", "text": "Before version 3, a profile was a directory ( profiles/<name>/profile.yml ) with its own robot, cameras and calibration.json . They don't show up until you move them: tandem profile migrate # tandem init also does this Each profile's task goes to profiles/<name>.yml and its trajectories to trajectories/<name>/ . The robot and cameras seed the rig (from the active profile) if rig.yml doesn't exist "}, {"title": "Phase planning (hitl)", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#phase-planning-hitl", "text": "Phase planning is on in the paper's profiles and in every new profile, and off in a profile with no hitl: block. The defaults match the paper. Some common changes: # Label failed human steps yourself, instead of excluding the trial from the dataset hitl : enabled : true on_verification_failure : label # When the planner can't plan a robot step, let a person do it (checked like a human step) hitl :"}, {"title": "Planning", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#planning", "text": "key default what it does enabled false When off, each attempt is one leg to the planner's goal, with no phases or checks. proposal_model gemini-2.5-pro Splits the task into phases and invents predicates and operators. vlm_model gemini-2.5-flash Answers checks, and names objects for tandem plan . max_attempts 3 Tries per proposal and per check, rejections fed back. Also the re-plan limit under repl"}, {"title": "Checks", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#checks", "text": "key default what it does verification_camera external Which camera's image the checks use: external , hand or perception (whichever camera perception uses). check_human_effects true After a human phase, check its add effects hold and delete effects don't. check_human_preconditions false Check a human phase's preconditions once, before the hand-off. check_tamp_preconditions false Before a robot leg"}, {"title": "Failures", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#failures", "text": "key default what it does verify_retries 1 Extra tries for a human phase that fails its check; the operator is told what's missing. \u2265 0. on_verification_failure exclude What happens when a check still fails after retries. exclude files the trial in failure/ as excluded, with no label. label lets your label decide. on_robot_phase_failure abort What happens when a robot phase can't be planned. abort "}, {"title": "Human executor", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#human-executor", "text": "key default what it does human_executor teleop Who does human phases ( choosing one ). human_executor_options {} Each executor's settings, keyed by its name. Checked at load. allow_unrecorded_human_phase false While recording, accept a human phase with no recording ( d , or a leg with no frames). --no-record always accepts."}, {"title": "The rig", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#the-rig", "text": "The rig holds this workstation's robot, camera and calibration settings. All profiles use it. tandem init writes it to ~/.config/tandem/rig.yml : version : 1 robot : type : fr3_robotiq host : 172.16.0.2 # the NUC cameras : perception : external hand : { serial : '14846828' } external : { serial : '32439448' } calibration : calibration.json planners : {} # a planner's machine settings, where they d"}, {"title": "Calibration file", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#calibration-file", "text": "calibration.json holds one pose per camera, keyed by serial: { \"14846828\" : { \"pose\" : [ 0.0266 , 0.0705 , -0.1392 , -0.4509 , -0.0045 , -1.5620 ]}, \"32439448\" : { \"pose\" : [ 0.1532 , -0.5827 , 0.4410 , -2.0843 , 0.0126 , 0.1964 ]} } pose is [x, y, z, roll, pitch, yaw] , in meters and radians ( xyz Euler angles). The wrist camera's pose is relative to the end effector ( ee_from_cam ). An external "}, {"title": "Related settings outside the rig", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#related-settings-outside-the-rig", "text": "TiPToP's scripts ( calibrate-wrist-cam , viz-calibration , cutamp-demo , \u2026) run through tandem runtime run or shell use your rig settings, through $TIPTOP_CONFIG and $TIPTOP_CALIBRATION . --raw uses tiptop's stock config instead. Teleop uses robot.host too; tandem passes it to the teleop driver. Gripper mask ( perception: hand only): make yours with tandem runtime run compute-gripper-mask (or pain"}, {"title": "Planner settings", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#planner-settings", "text": "A profile names its planner and that planner's settings for the task: planner : backend : tiptop # which planner options : # its task settings, checked by the planner at load tamp : num_particles : 512 Task settings go in the profile's planner.options . Machine settings (a server's address, a robot's ports) go in the rig under planners.<name> . If you put a key in the wrong file, tandem refuses it"}, {"title": "Switching planners", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#switching-planners", "text": "tandem planners use myplanner # the active profile now plans with it tandem planners use myplanner -o KEY = VALUE # also set an option it requires (repeatable) tandem planners default myplanner # the planner new profiles get use sets planner.backend and stashes the old planner's options in profiles/.planner-options/<profile>.<planner>.yml , restoring them when you switch back. It warns if the runt"}, {"title": "TiPToP options", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#tiptop-options", "text": "Machine settings live in rig.yml under planners.tiptop : tandem rig set planners.tiptop.robot.time_dilation_factor 0 .3 # arm at 30% speed tandem rig set planners.tiptop.perception.m2t2.url http://HOST:8123 tandem rig set planners.tiptop.perception.depth_smoothing_frames 1 The robot's address and type come from robot.host and robot.type . robot : key default what it does dof 7 Joint count. It must"}, {"title": "Surface-fitted placement", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#surface-fitted-placement", "text": "By default, cuTAMP can place an object anywhere in a surface's bounding box, at the top of it, such as on an open box's rim or a plate's edge. placement_support: true places only on observed level patches that fit the footprint: tamp : placement_support : true placement_fill_occluded : true # e.g. a box whose floor the camera can't see key default what it does placement_support false Turns it on. "}, {"title": "The three switches", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#the-three-switches", "text": "These tamp: keys are off unless set. The paper's five and every new profile turn them on, because every run in the paper used them. tamp : table_plane_support_vote : true disjoint_object_masks : true blend_stretch_to_caps : true key what it switches on table_plane_support_vote Pick the table among RANSAC's planes by the objects resting on each, instead of by every object within 3 cm above or below"}, {"title": "tandem settings and credentials", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#tandem-settings-and-credentials", "text": "tandem's own settings live in ~/.config/tandem/config.toml : tandem config list tandem config set ui.port 8800 tandem config set data_root /mnt/data/tandem tandem config edit # also: get KEY, path key default what it does active_profile none The profile commands act on ( tandem profile use ). tandem init sets cover-bread-rolls , and tandem profile create makes its profile active when none is. data"}, {"title": "Credentials", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#credentials", "text": "Credentials are stored in credentials.toml next to it, readable only by you: tandem config set-gemini-key # for phase planning and TiPToP's perception tandem config set-hf-token # for tandem export lerobot --push GEMINI_API_KEY or GOOGLE_API_KEY overrides the stored Gemini key . The Hugging Face token is looked up in order: stored, HF_TOKEN or HUGGING_FACE_HUB_TOKEN , then ~/.cache/huggingface/tok"}, {"title": "Directories", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#directories", "text": "These are the Linux defaults; other systems differ. directory default env var config: config.toml , credentials.toml , rig.yml , calibration.json ~/.config/tandem $TANDEM_CONFIG_DIR state: logs, session scratch ( Logs and session files ) ~/.local/state/tandem $TANDEM_STATE_DIR share: runtimes ~/.local/share/tandem $TANDEM_SHARE_DIR data: profiles, trajectories ~/tandem-data $TANDEM_DATA_ROOT TiPTo"}, {"title": "The planner runtime", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#the-planner-runtime", "text": "tandem planners install tiptop # build or update TiPToP's runtime (tandem init runs this) tandem planners info tiptop # pinned vs installed commits tandem planners install tiptop --force # refetch and rebuild everything TiPToP's runtime is a pixi environment (torch, cuRobo's CUDA kernels, cuTAMP, tiptop). The first build takes about 25 GB and 5\u201320 minutes, and asks before installing pixi ( --yes a"}, {"title": "Offline install", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#offline-install", "text": "If the workstation can't reach GitHub, bundle the sources on a machine that can: tandem planners bundle tiptop --out /media/usb/planner-sources # on a machine with network tandem planners install tiptop --sources /media/usb/planner-sources # on the workstation Use the same tandem version on both: commits are checked against their pins and files against the digest. $TANDEM_PLANNER_SOURCES can repla"}, {"title": "Installing", "page": "Configuration", "url": "/tandem/docs/configuration/#installing", "text": "tandem isn't on PyPI; install it from git ( Installation ). Plain pip install git+\u2026 needs a virtualenv on Debian and Ubuntu. Every install includes what tandem export lerobot needs ( av , pyarrow and huggingface_hub ). To develop tandem: pip install -e '.[dev]' in a virtualenv, then pytest -q ."}, {"title": "Concepts", "page": "Concepts", "url": "/tandem/docs/concepts/", "text": "Concepts Terms used throughout these docs."}, {"title": "Tasks and setup", "page": "Concepts", "url": "/tandem/docs/concepts/#tasks-and-setup", "text": "profile : one task, kept in one YAML file ( ~/tandem-data/profiles/<name>.yml by default). It holds the prompt, the phase-planning settings and the planner's settings. Its trajectories go in ~/tandem-data/trajectories/<name>/ . tandem init adds the paper's five tasks as profiles. rig : the robot, camera and calibration settings for this workstation, in ~/.config/tandem/rig.yml . All profiles use t"}, {"title": "Phase planning", "page": "Concepts", "url": "/tandem/docs/concepts/#phase-planning", "text": "phase planning : splitting an instruction into an ordered list of robot and human phases. It is on when a profile sets hitl.enabled: true . phase : one step, plus the atoms that must hold after it. An atom is a predicate applied to objects, such as On(bread, plate) . A robot phase becomes a goal for the planner. A human phase goes to a human executor. proposal : the model's list of phases. Repair "}, {"title": "Trials and recordings", "page": "Concepts", "url": "/tandem/docs/concepts/#trials-and-recordings", "text": "human executor : whatever carries out a human phase. Only teleop , a person driving the arm, ships with tandem. A hand-off lends the executor the arm and then takes it back. perception pass : one perceive call, which names the objects in the scene. One runs before each robot leg. trial : one attempt at a task. All its legs share a 16-hex-character trajectory_id . leg : one continuous recording, ma"}, {"title": "Planners", "page": "Concepts", "url": "/tandem/docs/concepts/#planners", "text": "runtime : a planner's pinned sources, and usually a pixi environment, built by tandem planners install . sidecar : a planner running as a subprocess that tandem drives over JSON lines, inside its runtime if it has one. DATAFARM : TiPToP tamp motion costs (a VAE manifold and RND novelty) that make planned motion resemble DROID teleop motion."}, {"title": "Command reference", "page": "Command reference", "url": "/tandem/docs/commands/", "text": "Command reference tandem <command> --help lists every flag. [profile] defaults to the active profile. Global options go first: tandem --debug collect shows full tracebacks, and --no-color (or NO_COLOR ) turns off color. --json works with doctor , plan , profile list|show , rig show , traj list|show , planners list|info|use|default , executors list|use , runtime status and config list ."}, {"title": "Setup", "page": "Command reference", "url": "/tandem/docs/commands/#setup", "text": "command what it does tandem init Checks, data directory, planner runtime and its servers , Gemini key , the rig , the paper's five and teleop. Moves older profiles . Skips steps already done. tandem doctor Runs every check and says how to fix what fails. Changes nothing. flag what it does init --viz-only Set up a laptop for reviewing only: no runtime or robot. init -y , --yes Accept every prompt. "}, {"title": "Profiles", "page": "Command reference", "url": "/tandem/docs/commands/#profiles", "text": "tandem profile list|show|use|edit|path|delete|create|migrate manages profiles , one YAML file each. flag what it does create NAME --prompt \"...\" A new profile: the paper's settings with your task. create NAME --from PROFILE A copy of another profile. create --use , --force Make it active; replace a profile of that name (its trajectories are kept). show --planner Show only what the planner receives"}, {"title": "Rig", "page": "Command reference", "url": "/tandem/docs/commands/#rig", "text": "tandem rig show|set|edit|path manages the robot, camera and calibration settings . set KEY VALUE takes one dotted key, such as robot.host , and null removes it. path --calibration prints where the extrinsics file is."}, {"title": "Planners and executors", "page": "Command reference", "url": "/tandem/docs/commands/#planners-and-executors", "text": "command what it does and its flags planners list , info NAME Every planner and its state; one planner's needs, pinned vs installed commits, goal language and settings. -p/--profile . planners install NAME Build or update its runtime . --sources DIR ( offline ), --force (refetch and rebuild), -y/--yes . planners remove NAME Delete its runtime only. planners use NAME Make a profile plan with it ( se"}, {"title": "Settings and runtime", "page": "Command reference", "url": "/tandem/docs/commands/#settings-and-runtime", "text": "tandem config list|get|set|edit|path|set-gemini-key|set-hf-token manages tandem's settings . set KEY VALUE takes one dotted key, such as ui.port . The set-* commands store a credential typed at a prompt, or read it with --stdin . set-gemini-key --key KEY also works, but leaves the key in your shell history. tandem runtime status|build|shell|python|run|clean|path works on the active profile's plann"}, {"title": "Servers", "page": "Command reference", "url": "/tandem/docs/commands/#servers", "text": "tandem servers install|status|start|stop manages the helper servers the planner calls: TiPToP 's M2T2 (grasps) and FoundationStereo (depth), at the URLs in the rig . tandem init builds them, and tandem collect starts any that are down and stops the ones it started when it ends. command what it does servers install Build their runtimes. --force (refetch, rebuild), --sources DIR , -y/--yes . servers"}, {"title": "Data format", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/", "text": "Data format This page describes what a trial leaves on disk, and where tandem writes its logs. Paths are the Linux defaults ( moving them ). For the terms used here, see Concepts ."}, {"title": "Reading an episode", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/#reading-an-episode", "text": "import json from pathlib import Path import numpy as np ep = Path ( \"~/tandem-data/trajectories/my-task/success/2026-09-25_14-03-12\" ) . expanduser () meta = json . loads (( ep / \"_meta.json\" ) . read_text ()) state = np . load ( ep / \"robot_state.npz\" ) print ( meta [ \"instruction\" ], meta [ \"n_frames\" ], \"frames at\" , meta [ \"fps\" ], \"fps\" ) print ( state [ \"joint_position\" ] . shape ) # (F, 7) "}, {"title": "Episode layout", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/#episode-layout", "text": "Each trial is one directory, under success/ or failure/ : ~/tandem-data/trajectories/<profile>/<status>/<YYYY-MM-DD_HH-MM-SS>/ \u251c\u2500\u2500 external_cam.mp4 external_cam_2.mp4 hand_cam.mp4 exterior 1, exterior 2 (optional), wrist \u251c\u2500\u2500 robot_state.npz per-frame arrays \u251c\u2500\u2500 _meta.json lineage, timing, and which leg and phase each frame belongs to \u251c\u2500\u2500 hitl.json the phase record \u251c\u2500\u2500 vlm/ model queries \u251c\u2500\u2500 tiptop"}, {"title": "robot_state.npz", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/#robot_statenpz", "text": "The file has one row per frame. The per-leg arrays are listed in the recording contract . An episode may also have: array shape what it is video_time [F] float64 Merged episodes only: the frame's time in the joined videos, in seconds. action_joint_velocity [F,7] The DROID joint-velocity action, if any leg recorded it (TiPToP does). For legs without it, teleop and policy legs copy cmd_joint_velocit"}, {"title": "_meta.json", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/#_metajson", "text": "{ \"trajectory_id\" : \"3f9c0a1b7d2e4c55\" , \"planner\" : \"tiptop\" , \"instruction\" : \"place the bread inside the box\" , \"n_frames\" : 912 , \"fps\" : 15 , \"cameras\" : { \"exterior_image_1_left\" : \"external_cam.mp4\" , \"wrist_image_left\" : \"hand_cam.mp4\" }, \"segments\" : [ { \"source\" : \"tamp\" , \"timestamp\" : \"2026-09-25_14-03-12\" , \"phase_index\" : 0 , \"video_start\" : 0.0 , \"video_stop\" : 31.4 }, { \"source\" : "}, {"title": "hitl.json", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/#hitljson", "text": "This file is written when phase planning is on and the trial has a plan. It goes into the primary leg, then beside the merged episode. A trial stopped at the label prompt keeps it in its primary leg in eval/ . A trimmed example, for a trial where the robot moves the bread and a person closes the lid: { \"instruction\" : \"put the bread in the box and close it\" , \"trajectory_id\" : \"3f9c0a1b7d2e4c55\" ,"}, {"title": "Top-level keys", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/#top-level-keys", "text": "key what it is instruction , trajectory_id , planner , human_executor The task as planned, the trial id, the planner, and hitl.human_executor . outcome , excluded success , failure , excluded or aborted . excluded is outcome == \"excluded\" . failure_stage invention , tamp_planning , tamp_execution , verification , human_policy or null . It is always the loop's, whatever the label. filed_under succe"}, {"title": "specification", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/#specification", "text": "The proposal's reading of the instruction. key what it is invented_predicates[] {name, types, instructions} . instructions is the classifier sentence, with {0} , {1} , \u2026 for arguments. human_operators[] {phase, name, args, signature, instance, preconditions, add_effects, delete_effects} for each human phase with an operator. Load one with tandem.planning.structs.HumanOperator.from_json . surfaces["}, {"title": "checks", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/#checks", "text": "key what it is human_preconditions , human_effects , tamp_preconditions , tamp_effects , plan_effects , verify_enforced , precondition_enforced , verify_final_phase The hitl switches as configured. initial_state_classified Whether the first image was classified. plan_effects_rechecked , plan_effects_warning Whether the contract check re-ran on the first image, and its finding (or null ). unchecked"}, {"title": "phases[k]", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/#phasesk", "text": "Every phase has index , executor , description , atoms and planned_by . The rest depends on the kind: kind extra keys Robot goal (the atoms given to the planner, in its spelling) and goal_description . Once planned: planning_seconds , plan_reused , task_plan (the operator sequence, if reported), and covers_phases (if one plan covered several). Human instructions and operator . Once run, carried_ou"}, {"title": "verifications[k]", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/#verificationsk", "text": "One entry per camera verdict, failing ones included: {atom, statement, holds, expected, satisfied, role, reason, phase} . role is effect , effect (deleted) or precondition . Read satisfied . holds is what the model saw and expected is what the plan wanted, so satisfied == (holds == expected) . For a delete effect, holds: true is the failure. Only the attempt that settled a phase is kept. For an ex"}, {"title": "vlm/", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/#vlm", "text": "vlm/ \u251c\u2500\u2500 001_task-plan_input.png \u251c\u2500\u2500 001_task-plan_output.png \u251c\u2500\u2500 002_classify-LidClosed-red_bin_input.png \u251c\u2500\u2500 002_classify-LidClosed-red_bin_output.png \u2514\u2500\u2500 index.jsonl This directory holds every model query of the trial. It is written when phase planning is on and hitl.save_vlm_io is true (the default): first into the session directory, then copied into the episode when it is filed. Each query le"}, {"title": "The events file", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/#the-events-file", "text": "{ \"event\" : \"rollout_saved\" , \"ts\" : 1790270037.40 , \"dir\" : \"\u2026/eval/2026-09-25_14-03-12\" , \"n_frames\" : 12 } { \"event\" : \"human_phase_verified\" , \"ts\" : 1790270101.12 , \"phase_index\" : 1 , \"attempt\" : 1 , \"ok\" : true , \"verdicts\" : [ \u2026 ]} { \"event\" : \"trial_outcome\" , \"ts\" : 1790270110.87 , \"outcome\" : \"excluded\" , \"failure_stage\" : \"verification\" , \"reason\" : \"\u2026\" } A session appends one JSON lin"}, {"title": "From the phase loop", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/#from-the-phase-loop", "text": "event payload rollout_start dir , phase_index , n_phases ; also movables and return_home if they were passed to the planner rollout_saved dir , n_frames phase_complete phase_index (the next one), n_phases : more phases remain phase_plan_failed reason , policy ( on_robot_phase_failure ), phase_index ( null with phase planning off) instruction_not_fully_represented unrepresented : [{clause, reason}]"}, {"title": "From the session", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/#from-the-session", "text": "Below, trial summary means trajectory_id , outcome , excluded , filed_under , failure_stage , reason and labeled . event payload session_start , session_end , awaiting_task None awaiting_label dir labeled , trial_excluded dir and the trial summary; labeled adds success trial_filed dir and the trial summary: the trial failed part-way or was aborted, and was filed unlabeled trial_unlabeled dir and t"}, {"title": "Session summary", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/#session-summary", "text": "A session's live state. The web UI gets it from GET /api/sessions/{id} , and tandem collect saves it in session-<id>.json . The common fields are below; there are more. field what it is id , profile , task The session. state , error , end_reason Where it is ( warming , rolling , awaiting_task , awaiting_human_phase , teleop_handoff , awaiting_label , stopped , failed , \u2026), and how it ended. labele"}, {"title": "Logs and session files", "page": "Data format", "url": "/tandem/docs/data/#logs-and-session-files", "text": "path what it holds ~/.local/state/tandem/logs/session-<id>.json Written when tandem collect ends (not for web UI sessions): summary (the session summary ) and logs (the last 4,000 log lines). ~/.local/state/tandem/sessions/<profile>/<id>/events.jsonl The events file . \u2026/<id>/vlm/<trajectory id>/ Each trial's model queries . This is the only copy if the trial recorded nothing. \u2026/<id>/perception/<le"}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/", "text": "Troubleshooting Start with tandem doctor . It runs every check and says how to fix what it finds. Logs and session files are described in Logs and session files ."}, {"title": "A preempt didn't stop the arm", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#a-preempt-didnt-stop-the-arm", "text": "Use the physical E-stop. It is the only thing that stops the arm at once. p stops the plan from sending further steps, but the motion already sent still finishes ( details )."}, {"title": "A camera won't open, or shows serial number 0", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#a-camera-wont-open-or-shows-serial-number-0", "text": "ps aux | grep -E 'tandem|tiptop' # find the process holding the camera Another process holds the camera. Right after a hand-off, TiPToP 's save workers free the cameras within a few seconds, so wait and retry first."}, {"title": "\"the ZED SDK is not installed \u2026, so ZED cameras will not open\"", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#the-zed-sdk-is-not-installed--so-zed-cameras-will-not-open", "text": "tandem planners install tiptop # after installing the ZED SDK; adds only what is missing The runtime was built without the ZED SDK, so it has no ZED Python API ( pyzed ). Install the ZED SDK first ( details )."}, {"title": "\"no camera extrinsics for serial(s) \u2026\"", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#no-camera-extrinsics-for-serials-", "text": "tandem rig show # check the serial is right tandem rig path --calibration # the file to add its entry to A camera in the rig has no entry in the rig's calibration.json , so the session won't start. Add the entry ( format and steps )."}, {"title": "The robot doesn't answer (\"robot control \u2026 TimeoutError\")", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#the-robot-doesnt-answer-robot-control--timeouterror", "text": "tandem rig show # the address tandem uses: robot.host tandem rig set robot.host NUC_ADDRESS # if that isn't the NUC's address TimeoutError : nothing answers at that address. ConnectionRefusedError : the NUC answers, but the shim isn't running. Start it ( Robot setup ). Teleop uses the same robot.host , so fixing it fixes both."}, {"title": "A perception server doesn't answer (\"foundation stereo depth server \u2026\", \"m2t2 grasp server \u2026\")", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#a-perception-server-doesnt-answer-foundation-stereo-depth-server--m2t2-grasp-server-", "text": "tandem servers status # installed? answering? tandem servers install # if it says \"not installed\" tandem servers start # starts it and waits; a failure names its log TiPToP asks both servers on every rollout ( Perception servers ). Sessions start local servers automatically, so this usually means the server isn't built, or it crashed while loading. The log is ~/.local/state/tandem/logs/server-<nam"}, {"title": "A trial was excluded", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#a-trial-was-excluded", "text": "tandem traj relabel <id> success --force # file it as a success anyway Usually a human phase failed its camera check, retries included. To see why, open the trial's hitl.json : each failed check in verifications has satisfied: false and the model's reason . The judged images are vlm/*_classify-* ( format ). If the checks themselves are wrong, set hitl.on_verification_failure: label while you tune "}, {"title": "The plan leaves part of the instruction out", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#the-plan-leaves-part-of-the-instruction-out", "text": "Put the missing object on the table, or reword the task. Usually a named object wasn't detected. tandem plan , the web UI and the session log ( NOT part of the plan \u2014 \u2026 ) list each dropped clause and why ( details )."}, {"title": "\"I did it\" is refused at a human step", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#i-did-it-is-refused-at-a-human-step", "text": "While recording, a human phase must run through its executor. Do one of these: Take the arm with t . If t isn't offered, tandem executors list says why. Set hitl.allow_unrecorded_human_phase: true . Run tandem collect --no-record ."}, {"title": "Teleop says \"the teleop runtime is not installed\"", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#teleop-says-the-teleop-runtime-is-not-installed", "text": "tandem executors install teleop Teleop's driver runs in an environment tandem builds. This builds it (a few minutes) and turns teleop on. If it says ZED cameras won't open, install the ZED SDK and run it again. It adds only what is missing."}, {"title": "The teleop driver can't reach the VR headset", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#the-teleop-driver-cant-reach-the-vr-headset", "text": "adb devices # the headset should be listed as \"device\" oculus_reader talks to the headset over adb ( sudo apt install adb ). Connect the headset by USB, turn on developer mode, and accept the prompt in the headset. A headset that has never run DROID teleop also needs the app ( Teleoperation )."}, {"title": "The planner shows as outdated", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#the-planner-shows-as-outdated", "text": "tandem planners install tiptop Its pinned sources moved, so the runtime was built from other commits ( why )."}, {"title": "The runtime build failed", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#the-runtime-build-failed", "text": "tandem planners install tiptop # after fixing the cause; finished steps are skipped The build prints its log path ( runtime-build-<time>.log ) first. The usual causes each have a tandem doctor row: missing nvcc , a torch/CUDA mismatch ( cuda runtime ), or a full disk ( disk space )."}, {"title": "A leg fails with \"No level patch of \u2026 is large enough\"", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#a-leg-fails-with-no-level-patch-of--is-large-enough", "text": "planner : options : tamp : placement_fill_occluded : true # a box wall or lid hides the floor # placement_flatness_tol: 0.012 # a noisy floor: raise it (default 0.008) # placement_support_required: false # last resort: use the bounding box With placement_support: true , no visible level patch of the goal surface fits the object's footprint plus placement_support_margin ( details ). The full messag"}, {"title": "\"vae_path does not exist\"", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#vae_path-does-not-exist", "text": "tandem planners install tiptop The paper's profiles name the DATAFARM checkpoint vae/checkpoints/vae_full_v2.pt , which the runtime install places. tandem looks for it beside the profile's file, then in the runtime. tandem doctor warns until the install has run."}, {"title": "A TAMP setting seems to do nothing", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#a-tamp-setting-seems-to-do-nothing", "text": "tandem profile show <name> --planner # exactly what the planner receives A key missing from that output never applied. tandem doctor 's tamp settings row flags a key that needs another, such as a placement_* key without placement_support: true . It isn't a typo; tandem refuses to load a profile with a misspelled key and suggests the closest one ( details )."}, {"title": "The videos won't scrub in the browser", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#the-videos-wont-scrub-in-the-browser", "text": "A proxy in front of tandem ui is probably dropping the Range header that /api/media/ needs. Configure it to pass Range through ( details )."}, {"title": "My profiles are gone after updating tandem", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#my-profiles-are-gone-after-updating-tandem", "text": "tandem profile migrate # or tandem init Profiles from before version 3 (a directory each, with its own cameras and robot) aren't listed until they are moved. Migrating moves them and sets up the rig from them, without deleting anything ( details )."}, {"title": "A profile's planner or executor isn't installed", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#a-profiles-planner-or-executor-isnt-installed", "text": "tandem planners use NAME # or install the missing planner's package tandem executors use NAME # add -p PROFILE for another profile tandem collect refuses such a profile, with no planner named '\u2026' or Unknown human executor '\u2026' . You can still browse, export and edit it."}, {"title": "A sidecar dies with \"No module named 'tandem_sidecar'\"", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "/tandem/docs/troubleshooting/#a-sidecar-dies-with-no-module-named-tandem_sidecar", "text": "Your environment's activation (for example pixi 's [activation.env] ) overwrote PYTHONPATH , dropping tandem's tandem_sidecar kit. Make it append to PYTHONPATH instead ( details )."}, {"title": "Adding a planner", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/", "text": "Adding a planner A planner is a Python package that tells tandem three things: what is in the scene ( perceive ), how to reach one goal ( plan ), and how to run that plan on the robot while recording it ( execute ). tandem does everything else: splitting the task into phases, teleop, checking a person's work and merging the legs. Working examples: src/tandem/planners/tiptop/ (the built-in planner)"}, {"title": "Quick start", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#quick-start", "text": "Generate a package, install it where tandem runs, and run its tests: tandem planners new shelfbot # creates ./tandem-shelfbot (--dir to change) cd tandem-shelfbot pipx inject tandem-tamp --editable . pytest # if tandem came from pipx python -m pytest # passes as generated tandem planners use shelfbot # the active profile now plans with it The generated planner plans in a stand-in world, so its tes"}, {"title": "Names", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#names", "text": "A planner's name uses lowercase letters, digits, _ and - , and starts with a letter."}, {"title": "A minimal planner", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#a-minimal-planner", "text": "This is the whole shape of a planner, trimmed from the scaffold. It can move a block onto a tray. import json , time , uuid from pathlib import Path from tandem.planners import ( Capabilities , ExecuteResult , Parameter , Planner , PlannerInfo , PlanResult , Predicate , SceneView , ) ON = Predicate ( \"On\" , ( Parameter ( \"obj\" , \"movable\" ), Parameter ( \"place\" , \"surface\" ))) class ShelfBot ( Pla"}, {"title": "The protocol", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#the-protocol", "text": "A session calls a planner's verbs in this order: factory.create(ctx) \u2192 require_ready() \u2192 warm() \u2192 ( perceive() \u2192 plan() \u2192 execute() )* robot legs \u2192 release_hardware() \u2026 reacquire_hardware() around every human leg \u2192 home() \u2192 close() when the session stops Subclass Planner , not tandem.planners.base.TampBackend . Planner supplies every verb except perceive , plan and execute . If a verb raises, the "}, {"title": "SceneView", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#sceneview", "text": "field meaning object_labels Every object seen, except the table. table_label The support surface. Defaults to table . surface_labels The objects that are surfaces. scene_id An opaque id that tandem passes back to plan . rgb_path An image of the scene. tandem splits the task and checks robot-leg preconditions from it. Without it, proposing a plan fails and the trial ends at invention . detected_goa"}, {"title": "PlanResult", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#planresult", "text": "Every field must be JSON-safe. field meaning ok , failure_reason Whether it planned, and why not. planning_seconds How long planning took. plan_handle An opaque id that tandem passes back to execute . task_plan The operators with object arguments, e.g. (\"Pick(bread)\", \"Place(bread, plate)\") . It goes into hitl.json and is never parsed. artifacts Role \u2192 path of each file written. skeleton , skeleto"}, {"title": "Capabilities", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#capabilities", "text": "Capabilities are everything phase planning knows about your planner. They are checked when the class is defined. A minimal planner shows the required fields. A planner usually adds a few more, as the scaffold does: CAPABILITIES = Capabilities ( ... , # the required fields, as in the minimal planner exclusive_arguments = { \"On\" : 0 }, # an object rests on one thing at a time moved_arguments = { \"On"}, {"title": "The Planner base class", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#the-planner-base-class", "text": "tandem.planners.Planner is an abstract base class, and it is also its own factory: the registry holds the class itself. class attribute required what it is info yes PlannerInfo(name, display_name, summary, homepage, requires, sources) , shown by tandem planners list and info . requires is free text. sources defaults to the recipe's. CAPABILITIES yes See Capabilities . recipe no A RuntimeRecipe . N"}, {"title": "Sidecars", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#sidecars", "text": "Use a sidecar when the planner needs torch, CUDA kernels, a camera SDK or a robot client. The planner then runs as a script in its own interpreter, and tandem talks to it over JSON lines. tandem planners new NAME --sidecar generates both halves. The tandem half declares the planner and says which script to launch: # tandem_armsim/planner.py -- runs in tandem's process; keep it light from tandem.pl"}, {"title": "What tandem does with a sidecar", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#what-tandem-does-with-a-sidecar", "text": "behaviour detail Launch Runs the runtime's python with pixi run (or tandem's interpreter without a runtime), from the runtime's working directory, in its own process group. Optional arguments movables , return_home and reuse_skeleton are sent only when declared. Passing an undeclared one is an error. Output Logs and stderr go to the session log. Events go to the events file (through on_event ). Cr"}, {"title": "Default timeouts", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#default-timeouts", "text": "verb seconds warm 900 perceive 300 plan 900 execute 1800 capture_frame , home , release_hardware , reacquire_hardware 180"}, {"title": "SidecarPlanner methods you can override", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#sidecarplanner-methods-you-can-override", "text": "method what it controls launch_command The command that starts the sidecar. launch_cwd Its working directory. launch_env Its environment. warm_args What warm sends. Defaults to output_dir , execute and record . on_event What happens to each event the sidecar sends. call(verb, **args) Not an override: calls a sidecar-only verb."}, {"title": "The tandem_sidecar kit", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#the-tandem_sidecar-kit", "text": "function what it does serve(handlers, verbs=None, on_exit=None) Answers requests until tandem says quit or closes stdin, then calls on_exit or the close handler. handlers is an object with one method per verb, or a verb \u2192 callable mapping. log(message, level=\"info\") Writes to the session log. Safe from any thread. event(name, **fields) Sends an event. The fields can't be named id , log or event . "}, {"title": "A runtime recipe", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#a-runtime-recipe", "text": "A planner that needs more than pip declares its runtime as data. tandem planners install NAME builds it, tandem planners list shows whether it is current, and tandem planners remove NAME deletes it. Here is a recipe with one pinned source, a pixi environment and one build step: from tandem.planners import BuildStep , PixiEnvironment , RuntimeRecipe , Source , SourcePin RECIPE = RuntimeRecipe ( pla"}, {"title": "Options and doctor rows", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#options-and-doctor-rows", "text": "A planner has two kinds of settings. Task settings live in each profile's planner.options and are declared in OPTIONS . Machine settings, such as a server's address or a robot's ports, live in rig.yml under planners.<name> and are declared in RIG_OPTIONS . from pydantic import BaseModel from tandem.core.errors import TandemError class _Options ( BaseModel , extra = \"forbid\" ): speed : float = 0.5 "}, {"title": "What people see", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#what-people-see", "text": "describe_options(profile, *, settings=None) returns an OptionsView with summary , sections , receives , receives_note and warnings . tandem profile show , the web editor and the session header show it. By default it lists each option as set. tandem profile show NAME --planner prints receives , which is exactly what the planner gets."}, {"title": "Doctor rows", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#doctor-rows", "text": "doctor_checks(profile, *, settings=None, probe_hardware=True) adds rows to tandem doctor . It returns tandem.core.probe.Check(name, state, detail, hint, group) rows, and by default returns none. state is probe.OK , WARN , FAIL or SKIP . With profile=None (the preflight of tandem init ), check only the machine. With probe_hardware=False ( --no-hardware ), touch no network or bus. A FAIL stops a ses"}, {"title": "Registering it", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#registering-it", "text": "The scaffold registers the planner with an entry point in pyproject.toml : [project.entry-points. \"tandem.planners\" ] arm = \"tandem_arm.planner:ArmPlanner\" # a Planner subclass, or any BackendFactory Then select it with tandem planners use arm , or planner: {backend: arm} in a profile. tandem planners default arm makes it the planner new profiles get ( Commands ). tandem looks up a name in this or"}, {"title": "The recording contract", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#the-recording-contract", "text": "execute records one leg of trial leg.trajectory_id . tandem's merge orders a trial's legs by their recording windows and joins them into one episode. When leg.record is set, save_dir must hold three things: _meta.json , robot_state.npz and the camera clips. save_dir/ \u251c\u2500\u2500 _meta.json \u251c\u2500\u2500 robot_state.npz \u251c\u2500\u2500 external_cam.mp4 external_cam_2.mp4 and hand_cam.mp4 too, if recorded \u2514\u2500\u2500 ... anything else i"}, {"title": "The conformance kit", "page": "Adding a planner", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-planner/#the-conformance-kit", "text": "tandem.planners.testing runs the whole protocol against your planner with no GPU, robot or session. Your package's test subclasses it: from tandem.planners.testing import PlannerConformance from tandem_arm.planner import ArmPlanner class TestArmPlanner ( PlannerConformance ): planner = ArmPlanner # a Planner subclass, a BackendFactory or a registered name records_legs = True # False checks only th"}, {"title": "Adding a human executor", "page": "Adding a human executor", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-human-executor/", "text": "Adding a human executor A human executor carries out a human phase and records it as one leg. It only decides how the phase is done: the plan decides which phase comes next, and the camera check decides whether it worked. tandem ships one executor, teleop . A package can add others, such as a learned policy. The code is in src/tandem/executors/ ."}, {"title": "Choosing one", "page": "Adding a human executor", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-human-executor/#choosing-one", "text": "tandem executors list # each executor: ready, needs setup (and what), or broken tandem executors use mypolicy # the active profile now uses it; -p PROFILE for another use sets the profile's hitl.human_executor . It warns if the executor isn't ready, and refuses an unknown or broken one ( HTTP API too). A profile that names a missing executor can't collect until use fixes it. It can still be browse"}, {"title": "Registering one", "page": "Adding a human executor", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-human-executor/#registering-one", "text": "A minimal executor that runs a policy, and the factory that registers it: # my_package/executor.py from tandem.executors import ExecutorFactory , HumanPhaseResult class PolicyExecutor : name = \"mypolicy\" segment_source = \"policy\" display_name = \"My policy\" summary = \"Runs a learned policy.\" def __init__ ( self , ctx ): self . ctx , self . _killed = ctx , False def run ( self , request , leg , * , "}, {"title": "ExecutorContext", "page": "Adding a human executor", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-human-executor/#executorcontext", "text": "create receives this once per session. field meaning profile , session_dir The validated profile; scratch space (not for recordings). rig The rig : the cameras a leg records from and the robot's address. None in a context built by hand; then read tandem.core.rig.load() . settings tandem's settings. None in a session: call tandem.core.settings.load() each leg, so tandem config set applies at the ne"}, {"title": "The protocol", "page": "Adding a human executor", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-human-executor/#the-protocol", "text": "Implement tandem.executors.HumanExecutor . Every member is required except close() . member contract name , display_name , summary The registered name, and what listings show. segment_source \"teleop\" or \"policy\" , matching the factory. It is written to each leg's _meta.json . run(request, leg, *, save_root, should_stop) Called once per attempt, at hand-over ( t or the UI). Record the phase, then r"}, {"title": "HumanPhaseRequest", "page": "Adding a human executor", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-human-executor/#humanphaserequest", "text": "field meaning phase_index , n_phases 0-based. n_phases is 0 when no plan is behind the phase (then leg.phase_index is None ). description , instructions , expected The phase in the model's words; what the person sees; what the camera check looks for. operator The magic operator, as HumanOperator.to_json() . None for a robot phase handed to a person ( on_robot_phase_failure: teleop ). attempt , mis"}, {"title": "HumanPhaseResult", "page": "Adding a human executor", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-human-executor/#humanphaseresult", "text": "HumanPhaseResult ( status , n_frames = 0 , leg_dir = None , leg_dirs = ()) status meaning what the loop does next done The phase was carried out. Checks its effects ( hitl keys ), with verify_retries more tries on failure. ended_by_operator The operator stopped it to move on, e.g. a step-limited policy. Keeps the leg and accepts the phase unchecked ( hitl.json ). aborted Cut off, or couldn't run ("}, {"title": "Custody", "page": "Adding a human executor", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-human-executor/#custody", "text": "Each human leg runs in four steps: backend.release_hardware() frees the robot and cameras. A failure only warns. executor.run(...) runs. It is skipped as aborted if a forced stop came during step 1. The leg is counted. It is labeled and merged even if step 4 fails. backend.reacquire_hardware() takes the robot back, unless run raised CustodyError . A failure ends the session. Any other exception fr"}, {"title": "The teleop executor", "page": "Adding a human executor", "url": "/tandem/docs/adding-a-human-executor/#the-teleop-executor", "text": "TeleopExecutor runs the DROID teleop driver, one process per leg. It needs: the teleop runtime, from tandem executors install teleop ( setup ). It holds the workstation side of DROID's TANDEM branch and oculus_reader , in their own environment; teleop.enabled ( keys ), which the install turns on; a VR headset and controller ( Meta Quest ). tandem sets DROID_NUC_IP and TIPTOP_*_CAMERA_ID for the dr"}];
